---
title: "Server BM25 on Qdrant 1.20"
description: "Run BM25 keyword search on a Qdrant 1.20 full-text index with QUERY BM25 — no sparse vectors, no embedding step, per-query k and b."
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

Qdrant 1.20 can score BM25 directly on a full-text payload index. QQL 1.9
exposes that as `QUERY BM25`: the server tokenizes, tracks document lengths,
and ranks. There is no sparse vector to store and nothing to embed.

{% aside type="note" title="Two BM25 paths" %}
**Server BM25** (`QUERY BM25 … USING <field>`) needs remote Qdrant ≥ 1.20 and
scores a payload field. **Client sparse BM25** (`QUERY TEXT … USING sparse` /
`USING bm25`) encodes text into a sparse vector on the client and still runs
everywhere, including edge and Qdrant < 1.20. A query leg uses exactly one
of them; QQL never scores the same leg twice.
{% /aside %}

## When to prefer it

Use server BM25 on remote Qdrant 1.20+ when the text already lives in the
payload:

- No sparse named vector and no write-path BM25 encoder.
- The server owns `avg_len`, so it stays correct as the corpus changes.
- `k` and `b` are set per query instead of baked into stored vectors.

Keep the [client sparse path](/docs/guides/hybrid-retrieval/) on edge, on
servers older than 1.20, and on collections that already rely on a sparse
vector. To move an existing sparse setup over, follow
[Migrate sparse BM25 to server BM25](/docs/guides/migrate-sparse-bm25/).

## Create a BM25 text index

BM25 scoring is an option on a `text` payload index:

{% qqlExample title="BM25-scored text index" %}
```sql
CREATE INDEX ON COLLECTION books FOR title TYPE text
  WITH (scoring = 'bm25', tokenizer = 'word', lowercase = true);
```
{% /qqlExample %}

For source code, use the 1.20 `code` tokenizer, which splits identifiers
such as `camelCase` and `snake_case`:

{% qqlExample title="BM25 index for code search" %}
```sql
CREATE INDEX ON COLLECTION repo FOR body TYPE text
  WITH (scoring = 'bm25', tokenizer = 'code', lowercase = true);
```
{% /qqlExample %}

Tokenizer, stemmer, and stopword options belong to the index, not the query.

## Query with QUERY BM25

`USING` names the **payload field** that carries the BM25 index — not a
vector.

{% qqlExample title="Server BM25 query" %}
```sql
QUERY BM25 'time travel' FROM books USING title LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

Tune saturation (`k`) and length normalization (`b`) per query. Omitted
values use Qdrant's defaults, `k = 1.2` and `b = 0.75`:

{% qqlExample title="Per-query k and b" %}
```sql
QUERY BM25 'time travel' FROM books USING title PARAMS (k = 1.2, b = 0.75) LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

`PARAMS` on `QUERY BM25` only takes `k` and `b`. Tenant or corpus
scoping for server BM25 comes from the full-text index and ordinary
`WHERE` filters — not from sparse-vector `idf`.

## Hybrid: dense + server BM25

Each `PREFETCH` leg is routed on its own. The dense leg embeds with
`QUERY TEXT`; the lexical leg is a server BM25 `text` query. RRF fuses them:

{% qqlExample title="Dense + server BM25 with RRF" %}
```sql
WITH
  dense AS (QUERY TEXT 'time travel' USING dense LIMIT 50),
  lexical AS (QUERY BM25 'time travel' USING title PARAMS (k = 1.2, b = 0.75) LIMIT 50)
QUERY FUSION RRF FROM books PREFETCH (dense, lexical) LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

The rest of [Hybrid retrieval](/docs/guides/hybrid-retrieval/) — RRF vs DBSF,
per-leg limits, `rrf_weights` — applies unchanged.

## Version gate

`QUERY BM25` and `scoring = 'bm25'` fail closed **before** any request is
sent:

| Target | Result |
|---|---|
| Remote Qdrant ≥ 1.20 | Server BM25 |
| Remote Qdrant < 1.20 | Typed QQL version error, not a raw Qdrant 400 |
| qdrant-edge | Typed edge-unsupported error; use `QUERY TEXT … USING sparse` |
| Field without a BM25-scored text index | Typed validation error naming the `CREATE INDEX` to run |

Client BM25 options — `bm25_k1`, `bm25_b`, `bm25_avg_len`,
`setBm25Params`, language and tokenizer knobs — only shape the client sparse
encoder. They never change a `QUERY BM25` leg. See
[Edge models](/docs/edge/models/) for the client path.
